---
import DocLayout from "../layouts/DocLayout.astro";
const headings = [
  { label: "Global options", href: "#global-options" },
  { label: "scrape", href: "#scrape" },
  { label: "search", href: "#search" },
  { label: "map", href: "#map" },
  { label: "crawl", href: "#crawl" },
  { label: "benchmark", href: "#benchmark" },
  { label: "mcp", href: "#mcp" },
  { label: "Output and errors", href: "#output-and-errors" },
];
---

<DocLayout
  title="CLI reference"
  description="Use the lightweight scorch executable to call a running API for search, extraction, discovery, crawling, benchmarking, and MCP."
  headings={headings}
  previous={{ label: "Configuration", href: "/configuration/" }}
  next={{ label: "HTTP API", href: "/api/" }}
>
  <div class="callout">
    <code>scorch</code> is an HTTP client. Start <code>scorchd</code> first, or point
    the client at an existing service with <code>SCORCH_API_URL</code>.
  </div>

  <h2 id="global-options">Global options</h2>
  <pre><code>scorch [--api-url URL] &lt;COMMAND&gt;</code></pre>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Default</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead
      ><tbody>
        <tr
          ><td><code>--api-url &lt;URL&gt;</code></td><td
            ><code>http://127.0.0.1:3000</code></td
          ><td
            >Scorch HTTP API base URL. Also read from <code>SCORCH_API_URL</code
            >.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>-h, --help</code></td><td>—</td><td
            >Print top-level or subcommand help.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>-V, --version</code></td><td>—</td><td
            >Print the client version.</td
          ></tr
        >
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The global option may appear before the subcommand:</p>
  <pre><code>scorch --api-url https://scorch.internal.example search "rust"</code></pre>

  <h2 id="scrape">scorch scrape</h2>
  <pre><code>scorch scrape [OPTIONS] &lt;URL&gt;</code></pre>
  <p>
    Fetch or render one public page and print the API response as formatted
    JSON.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Default</th><th>Values</th></tr></thead
      ><tbody>
        <tr
          ><td><code>--format</code></td><td><code>markdown</code></td><td
            >Comma-separated: <code>markdown</code>, <code>html</code>, <code
              >text</code
            >, <code>links</code>, <code>metadata</code>, <code>screenshot</code
            >.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>--render</code></td><td><code>auto</code></td><td
            ><code>auto</code>, <code>always</code>, or <code>never</code>.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>--browser</code></td><td>service default</td><td
            ><code>obscura</code> or <code>chromium</code>; must be allowed by
            server policy.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>--timeout-ms</code></td><td><code>30000</code></td><td
            >Total scrape deadline from 100 to 120,000 ms.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>--wait-for-ms</code></td><td><code>0</code></td><td
            >Explicit wait after load; server caps it at 60,000 ms.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>--full-content</code></td><td>off</td><td
            >Keep the full page instead of focusing extraction on main content.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>--full-page-screenshot</code></td><td>off</td><td
            >Capture the complete page instead of the viewport.</td
          ></tr
        >
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <pre><code># Direct-first Markdown and links
scorch scrape https://example.com --format markdown,links

# Force JavaScript rendering with an allowed backend
scorch scrape https://example.com/app \
  --render always \
  --browser obscura \
  --wait-for-ms 500

# Return a full-page PNG data URI
scorch scrape https://example.com \
  --format screenshot \
  --full-page-screenshot</code></pre>

  <h2 id="search">scorch search</h2>
  <pre><code>scorch search [OPTIONS] &lt;QUERY&gt;</code></pre>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Default</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead
      ><tbody>
        <tr
          ><td><code>--limit</code></td><td><code>5</code></td><td
            >Number of results, from 1 to 20.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>--scrape</code></td><td>off</td><td
            >Enrich each result through the guarded scrape pipeline.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>--country</code></td><td><code>us</code></td><td
            >Country hint sent to applicable engines.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>--language</code></td><td><code>en</code></td><td
            >Language hint sent to applicable engines.</td
          ></tr
        >
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <pre><code>scorch search "rust async runtime" --limit 8
scorch search "web extraction" --country gb --language en --scrape</code></pre>
  <p>
    There is no engine option. Metasearch routing is controlled exclusively by <code
      >scorchd</code
    >.
  </p>

  <h2 id="map">scorch map</h2>
  <pre><code>scorch map [OPTIONS] &lt;URL&gt;</code></pre>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Default</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead
      ><tbody>
        <tr
          ><td><code>--limit</code></td><td><code>100</code></td><td
            >Maximum normalized URLs; server maximum is 1,000.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>--include-subdomains</code></td><td>off</td><td
            >Allow discovered links on subdomains of the requested site.</td
          ></tr
        >
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <pre><code>scorch map https://docs.example.com --limit 300 --include-subdomains</code></pre>

  <h2 id="crawl">scorch crawl</h2>
  <pre><code>scorch crawl [OPTIONS] &lt;URL&gt;</code></pre>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Default</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead
      ><tbody>
        <tr
          ><td><code>--limit</code></td><td><code>20</code></td><td
            >Maximum pages; server maximum is 100.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>--max-depth</code></td><td><code>2</code></td><td
            >Discovery depth; server maximum is 5.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>--concurrency</code></td><td><code>4</code></td><td
            >Per-job concurrency, capped by the service global limit.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>--wait</code></td><td>off</td><td
            >Poll until the job completes, is cancelled, or fails.</td
          ></tr
        >
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <pre><code># Start and return the job summary immediately
scorch crawl https://example.com --limit 40 --max-depth 3

# Wait and print the first result page at terminal state
scorch crawl https://example.com --limit 20 --wait</code></pre>
  <h3 id="crawl-status">Read crawl status</h3>
  <pre><code>scorch crawl-status &lt;ID&gt; --cursor 0 --page-size 10</code></pre>
  <p>
    <code>--cursor</code> defaults to 0 and <code>--page-size</code> defaults to 10.
    API page sizes range from 1 to 50. Use <code>nextCursor</code> from a response
    to continue.
  </p>
  <h3 id="crawl-cancel">Cancel and remove a crawl</h3>
  <pre><code>scorch crawl-cancel &lt;ID&gt;</code></pre>
  <p>
    Cancellation removes the job. A later status request for that ID returns not
    found.
  </p>

  <h2 id="benchmark">scorch benchmark</h2>
  <pre><code>scorch benchmark [--runs N] &lt;URLS&gt;...</code></pre>
  <p>
    The benchmark command calls the configured API, not the engine directly. It
    runs scrape requests using fetch, auto, and browser modes and prints
    comparative JSON.
  </p>
  <pre><code>scorch benchmark --runs 5 \
  https://example.com \
  https://example.org</code></pre>
  <p>
    The default is three runs per input. Use optimized <code>scorchd</code> builds
    when measurements need to represent production behavior.
  </p>

  <h2 id="mcp">scorch mcp</h2>
  <pre><code>SCORCH_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3000 scorch mcp</code></pre>
  <p>
    Starts a Model Context Protocol server over stdio. It is designed to be
    launched by an MCP host rather than used interactively. Every tool call is
    forwarded over HTTP to the configured API. See the <a href="/mcp/"
      >MCP integration guide</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2 id="output-and-errors">Output and errors</h2>
  <p>
    Normal command results are pretty-printed JSON on stdout. Diagnostics are
    written to stderr. This makes piping reliable:
  </p>
  <pre><code>scorch search "rust scraping" --limit 10 | jq '.results[].url'</code></pre>
  <p>
    HTTP errors include the API error code and message. Every API response
    carries an <code>x-request-id</code> header; service logs use the same ID for
    correlation.
  </p>
</DocLayout>
